 "refs": [
  {
   "title": "Present continuous: stare + -ando / -endo",
   "html": "<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"ref\">\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>stare</th><th>-are → -ando</th><th>-ere → -endo</th><th>-ire → -endo</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">io</td><td>sto</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">tu</td><td>stai</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">lui / lei / Lei</td><td>sta</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">noi</td><td>stiamo</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">voi</td><td>state</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">loro</td><td>stanno</td><td>cucinando</td><td>leggendo</td><td>dormendo</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"reftip\">Only stare changes. The -ando / -endo word stays the same for everyone.</p>\n<h3 class=\"refh\">The three odd ones</h3>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>fare</b> → facendo · <b>bere</b> → bevendo · <b>dire</b> → dicendo<br>Most other irregulars are regular here: andando, uscendo, venendo, scegliendo. -isc- verbs drop the -isc-: capendo, finendo.</p>\n<h3 class=\"refh\">Now, or later?</h3>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Happening right now</b> (or ongoing for a while): stare + -ando / -endo. <i>Sto mangiando.</i><br><b>Later today, tonight, tomorrow</b>: plain present. <i>Mangio più tardi.</i> English says \"I'm eating later\", Italian doesn't.<br>With <b>piacere</b>, stare agrees with what you like: mi <b>sta</b> piacendo il vino, mi <b>stanno</b> piacendo i tortellini.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "The courses",
   "html": "<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"ref\">\n<thead><tr><th>Course</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">antipasti</td><td>appetizers, freddi or caldi. Not salad.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">primi</td><td>pasta, risotto, soup, polenta, gnocchi</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">secondi</td><td>meat, fish or poultry</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">contorni</td><td>sides: crudi, cotti, grigliati. Salad goes here.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">dolci</td><td>tiramisù, gelato, sorbetto, panna cotta</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">bevande</td><td>vino, espresso, digestivo (limoncello, grappa, amaro)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Notes on the handouts",
   "html": "<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Formal with the waiter.</b> In the dialogue Maria asks the waitress <i>Cosa ci consigli?</i> and Leo says <i>tieni il resto</i>. Both are tu. With a server, use Lei: <b>Cosa ci consiglia?</b> and <b>Tenga il resto</b>. Leo's <i>Ci porti…</i> is already the correct formal form.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Sì needs its accent.</b> The dialogue writes <i>Si</i> for yes several times. Yes is <b>sì</b>; si without an accent is a pronoun.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>gli zuccheri, not i zuccheri.</b> Words starting with z take lo / gli.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Saltimbocca alla romana</b> is already veal, so \"il vitello al saltimbocca\" is redundant. On a menu: <i>saltimbocca alla romana</i>.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>dell'insalata mista</b> means \"some mixed salad\", not \"the\".</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Handout 1.1 example.</b> <i>Stasera stiamo bevendo per festeggiare</i> breaks the handout's own rule: for tonight, plain present (<i>Stasera beviamo…</i>) unless you're drinking as you say it.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Exercise 1</b> says <i>di fronte la televisione</i>; it should be <b>di fronte alla</b> televisione.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Customs handout:</b> fettuccine (not fettuccini); \"an antipasto\" (singular). <b>Menu:</b> tiramisù takes an accent.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\"><b>Table:</b> in a restaurant ask for <b>un tavolo</b>. La tavola is the table set for a meal.</p>\n<p class=\"reftip\">The Present Continuous Exercises and your ten Simulation sentences are left off these cards on purpose: they're yours to write. Bring them and we'll role-play the restaurant.</p>"
  }
 ]
}